\subsection{Downstream Utility}
\label{sec:results-downstream}

Depth-aware data curation does not improve downstream language model quality.
Fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on five data variants (3 seeds each) shows that data quantity dominates depth composition: random subsampling (ppl\,=\,2.367, 95\% CI [2.367, 2.367]) outperforms depth-stratified selection (ppl\,=\,2.387, 95\% CI [2.387, 2.388]) at equal sample count; $\Delta$\,=\,0.020, $p$\,=\,0.25 by paired permutation test ($n$\,=\,3 seeds; minimum achievable $p$ for this sample size).
Cross-seed variance is below 0.001 for all variants, so the ranking is stable but the test is underpowered (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Shape stationarity hypothesis.}
Upper-tail features remain near-stationary because the softmax architecture and sampling hyperparameters that determine distributional shape are fixed across depths; recursive generation shifts context toward high-probability regions (reducing entropy mean) without altering the generation mechanism itself.
This predicts stationarity until the model reaches its fixed-point distribution, a regime not observed at chain length $\leq$4.
Sampling ablation supports this: depth signal is robust at $t \in [0.5, 0.7]$ (${\leq}0.3$\,pp degradation) but breaks down at $t = 1.0$ ($-6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}), where sampling noise dominates distributional shape.

\paragraph{Prompt-type dependence.}
Continuation chains yield the strongest signal (F1\,=\,0.781 vs.\ 0.730 for elaboration), with cross-type transfer at 0.753 (Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}).
Semantic-preservation constraints attenuate drift, consistent with \citet{broken_telephone}.

\paragraph{Practical applicability.}
At the document level, gen-1 per-class F1\,=\,0.63 (37\% misclassification) is insufficient for automated filtering but suitable for flagging; at the corpus level, error scales as $O(1/\sqrt{n})$, making aggregate depth composition estimates reliable.
A detailed error analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis}) confirms that errors concentrate in shallow gen-2+ samples where distributional drift is insufficient, consistent with DDR predictions.

\paragraph{Relation to model collapse.}
Model collapse \cite{curse_of_recursion,strong_model_collapse} describes degradation when models are \emph{retrained} on their own outputs; our inference-chain drift occurs without parameter updates, representing an early, reversible stage of the same narrowing.
Topic drift accounts for $<$9\% of feature variance ($R^2 = 0.082$), confirming it mediates rather than confounds depth signal.

\paragraph{Scope boundaries.}
Three classes represent the practical granularity ceiling: 5-class LOGO F1 drops to $\approx$0.50, with gen-2/gen-3 inseparable (Appendix~\ref{app:finer-depth}); QLoRA fine-tuning recovers 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 (Appendix~\ref{app:qlora}), confirming signal beyond summary statistics.
Under text perturbation, the 7-feature ensemble achieves the highest unperturbed F1 with sublinear degradation; notably, LLM-simulated sentence regeneration (50\% edit distance) causes comparable degradation to 5\% random word replacement, confirming that depth signal captures generative structure rather than surface statistics (Appendix~\ref{app:human-edit}).
Instruction-tuned models show substantially weaker fingerprints (OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1\,=\,0.338 vs.\ 0.764 base), justifying our restriction to base models.
